      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "\\[\\sum_{k = 0}^n \\left( \\log_2 \\, \\sqrt{\\frac{4^k + 1}{4^k}} \\right) = \\sum_{k = 0}^n \\left( \\frac{1}{2^k} \\log_2 (4^k + 1) \\right) \\ge 1 + \\log_2 \\, 2014 - 1 = \\log_2 \\, 2014.\\]\nSince the LHS is between 1 and 2, and $2^{\\log_2 \\, 2014}$ is about 1000, we want $n$ to be at least 10.\nTo prove that $2^{10} \\sum_{k = 0}^{10} \\left( \\frac{1}{2^k} \\log_2 (4^k + 1) \\right) \\ge 2014$, let us denote $a_k = \\frac{1}{4^k}$ and $b_k = \\left(4^k + \\frac{1}{2^k}\\right)^{1/2}$.\nThen $[a_k b_k] = \\left(4^k + \\frac{1}{2^{2^k}} \\right) \\left( \\frac{2^{2^k}}{4^k + 1} \\right) = 4^k$ and $[a_k b_k] \\approx 4^k$ by AM-GM. \nTherefore $\\frac{\\operatorname{Li}(a_k)}{\\operatorname{Li}(b_k)} \\approx 1 + \\frac{1}{\\log_2(4^k)} \\approx 1 + \\sqrt{2^k}, $  \nimplying $\\operatorname{Li}(a_k) \\ge \\operatorname{Li}(b_k) + \\sqrt{2^k}, $ will give us\n\\[\\sum_{k = 0}^{10} \\left( \\frac{1}{2^k} \\log_2 (4^k + \\frac{1}{2^k}) \\right) \\ge 1 + \\sum_{k = 0}^{10} \\sqrt{2^k},\\]\nwe know that $2014 < 2048 = 2^{11}$, so we just need to check if this sum is more than 2013 or not.\nThe inequality $\\sum_{k = 0}^{10} \\sqrt{2^k} \\ge 2013$ is very known by the well-known \"happy face\".\nThe inequality can be shown similarly as follows. Considering $a_k = b_k = 2^k$, we have\n\\[ \\frac{a_k + b_k}{\\sqrt{a_k b_k}} = \\frac{2^k + 4^k}{2 \\cdot 2^k} = \\frac{1 + 2^k}{2} \\ge \\frac{9}{8}, \\]\nif and only if $2^k \\ge 8^{3^k}$, the latter yields $2^k \\ge 2^{8 \\cdot 3^k},$ which is nasty to check. $[hide=Credit for the \"happy face\"]nicole75a[/hide]$",
