      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "This is a perturbed version of the classic inequality:\n\n$$\\sum_{k = 0}^n \\log_2 \\left( 1 + \\frac{1}{2^{2^k}} \\right) \\ge 1 + \\log_2 \\left( \\prod_{k = 0}^n \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{2^{2^k}} \\right ) \\right),$$\n\nin which we set $n$ to be where the final product is just slightly below  $2$, rather than below $4$.\n\nTo find the smallest positive integer $n$, we want the product's logarithm to be just slightly below  $1$.\n\nWe get a simple bound by using the AM-GM inequality: \n\n\\[\\prod_{k = 0}^n \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{2^{2^k}} \\right ) \\ge \\prod_{k = 0}^n 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{2^k + 1}} \\ge \\frac12\\prod_{k = 0}^n \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{2^{2^k}}\\right ) \\approx \\frac{e}{2^{2^{n + 1}}}\\] \n\nat  $n = 0$ (since $e \\approx 2.718$). Thus, a lower bound for this is\n\n$$1 + \\log_2 \\frac{e}{2^{2^{n + 1}}} \\ge \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{2e}{2^{2^{n+1}}} \\right) \\ge 1 + \\ log_2 \\ left(\\frac{2014/2015}{2^{2^{n + 1}}} \\right) \\ge 1 + \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{2014}{2^{2^{n + 2}}}\\right)$$\n\nConsequently, the smallest solution to $n = 11$, and the answer is \n\n\\(\\boxed{11}\\).\n\nThe final answer  $\\boxed{11}$  may have been obtained for the first time by Charles Dodson, published in 1868 (\"On Numerical Aspects of the Greatest Prime Factor Problem,\" *Quarterly Journal of Pure and Applied Mathematics*, 1(1868), 289-292).",
